(; constants used by Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.MeanValue, declared with their translated types ;)

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__smulRightL__app : (a1 : Set) -> (a2 : Set) -> (a3 : Set) -> (a4 : Elem (StrongDual X X)) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) G (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X G)).

(; doc: The Landau notation `f =o[l] g` where `f` and `g` are two functions on a type `α` and `l` is a filter on `α`, means that eventually for `l`, `‖f‖` is bounded by an arbitrarily small constant multiple of `‖g‖`. In other words, `‖f‖ / ‖g‖` tends to `0` along `l`, modulo division by zero issues that are avoided by this definition. ;)
(; //LEAN:Asymptotics.IsLittleO ;)
Asymptotics__IsLittleO : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> E)) -> (g : (Elem X -> F)) -> Prop.

(; doc: See also `Continuous.comp_continuousOn'` using the form `fun y ↦ g (f y)` instead of `g ∘ f`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Continuous.comp_continuousOn ;)
Continuous__comp_continuousOn : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Y)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Z)) -> (g : (Elem Y -> Z)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (hg : Proof (Continuous Y Z g)) -> (hf : Proof (ContinuousOn X Y f s)) -> Proof (ContinuousOn X Z (Function__comp X Y Z g f) s).

(; //LEAN:Continuous.continuousAt ;)
Continuous__continuousAt : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Y)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Continuous X Y f)) -> Proof (ContinuousAt X Y f x2).

(; doc: A function between topological spaces is continuous at a point `x₀` if `f x` tends to `f x₀` when `x` tends to `x₀`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousAt ;)
ContinuousAt : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:ContinuousAt.comp ;)
ContinuousAt__comp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Y)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Z)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x3 : Elem X) -> (g : (Elem Y -> Z)) -> (hg : Proof (ContinuousAt Y Z g (f x3))) -> (hf : Proof (ContinuousAt X Y f x3)) -> Proof (ContinuousAt X Z (Function__comp X Y Z g f) x3).

(; //LEAN:ContinuousAt.continuousWithinAt ;)
ContinuousAt__continuousWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Y)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (ContinuousAt X Y f x2)) -> Proof (ContinuousWithinAt X Y f s x2).

(; doc: Continuous linear maps between modules. We only put the type classes that are necessary for the definition, although in applications `M` and `M₂` will be topological modules over the topological ring `R`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap ;)
ContinuousLinearMap : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.continuous ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__continuous : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X1)) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom X X1)) -> (X2 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X2)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X2)) -> (X3 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X3)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X3)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X X2)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X1 X3)) -> (f : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X1 x2 X2 X3)) -> Proof (Continuous X2 X3 f).

(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.ext_ring ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__ext_ring : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X1)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X1)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (f : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X X1)) -> (g : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X X1)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X1 (f 1) (g 1))) -> Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X X1) f g).

(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.nnnorm_toSpanSingleton ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__nnnorm_toSpanSingleton : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (x3 : Elem E) -> Proof (Eqt NNReal (NNNorm__nnnorm (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X E) (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X E x3)) (NNNorm__nnnorm E x3)).

(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.norm_toSpanSingleton ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__norm_toSpanSingleton : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (x3 : Elem E) -> Proof (Eq (Norm__norm (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X E) (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X E x3)) (Norm__norm E x3)).

(; doc: Given an element `x` of a topological space `M` over a semiring `R`, the natural continuous linear map from `R` to `M` by taking multiples of `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.toSpanSingleton ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x5 : Elem X1) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X X1).

(; doc: A function between topological spaces is continuous on a subset `s` when it's continuous at every point of `s` within `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousOn ;)
ContinuousOn : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function between topological spaces is continuous at a point `x₀` within a subset `s` if `f x` tends to `f x₀` when `x` tends to `x₀` while staying within `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousWithinAt ;)
ContinuousWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: Convexity of sets. ;)
(; //LEAN:Convex ;)
Convex : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Differentiable 𝕜 f` means that `f` is differentiable at any point. ;)
(; //LEAN:Differentiable ;)
Differentiable : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f` is differentiable at a point `x` if it admits a derivative there (possibly non-unique). ;)
(; //LEAN:DifferentiableAt ;)
DifferentiableAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableAt.hasDerivAt ;)
DifferentiableAt__hasDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (x3 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (DifferentiableAt X X F f x3)) -> Proof (HasDerivAt X F f (deriv X F f x3) x3).

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableAt.hasFDerivAt ;)
DifferentiableAt__hasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (h : Proof (DifferentiableAt X E F f x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f (fderiv X E F f x7) x7).

(; doc: `DifferentiableOn 𝕜 f s` means that `f` is differentiable within `s` at any point of `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DifferentiableOn ;)
DifferentiableOn : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableOn.sub ;)
DifferentiableOn__sub : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (g : (Elem E -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (hf : Proof (DifferentiableOn X E F f s)) -> (hg : Proof (DifferentiableOn X E F g s)) -> Proof (DifferentiableOn X E F (minus f g) s).

(; doc: A function `f` is differentiable at a point `x` within a set `s` if it admits a derivative there (possibly non-unique). ;)
(; //LEAN:DifferentiableWithinAt ;)
DifferentiableWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableWithinAt.hasDerivWithinAt ;)
DifferentiableWithinAt__hasDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (x3 : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (h : Proof (DifferentiableWithinAt X X F f s x3)) -> Proof (HasDerivWithinAt X F f (derivWithin X F f s x3) s x3).

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableWithinAt.hasFDerivWithinAt ;)
DifferentiableWithinAt__hasFDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (DifferentiableWithinAt X E F f s x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f (fderivWithin X E F f s x7) s x7).

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mp : α → β` is the induced "cast" operation, mapping elements of `α` to elements of `β`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mp` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mp ;)
Eq__mp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `Eq.refl a : a = a` is reflexivity, the unique constructor of the equality type. See also `rfl`, which is usually used instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.refl ;)
Eq__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; //LEAN:Exists.imp ;)
Exists__imp : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (q : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (a2 => if (p a2) (q a2)))) -> (h1 : Proof (exists X (a1 => p a1))) -> Proof (exists X (a => q a)).

(; doc: A filter `F` on a type `α` is a collection of sets of `α` which contains the whole `α`, is upwards-closed, and is stable under intersection. We do not forbid this collection to be all sets of `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter ;)
Filter : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: `f.Eventually p` or `∀ᶠ x in f, p x` mean that `{x | p x} ∈ f`. E.g., `∀ᶠ x in atTop, p x` means that `p` holds true for sufficiently large `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.Eventually ;)
Filter__Eventually : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (f : Elem (Filter X)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Filter.Eventually.mono ;)
Filter__Eventually__mono : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (q : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (f : Elem (Filter X)) -> (hp : Proof (Filter__Eventually X (x2 => p x2) f)) -> (hq : Proof (forall X (x1 => if (p x1) (q x1)))) -> Proof (Filter__Eventually X (x => q x) f).

(; doc: `f.Frequently p` or `∃ᶠ x in f, p x` mean that `{x | ¬p x} ∉ f`. E.g., `∃ᶠ x in atTop, p x` means that there exist arbitrarily large `x` for which `p` holds true. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.Frequently ;)
Filter__Frequently : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (f : Elem (Filter X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Function composition, usually written with the infix operator `∘`. A new function is created from two existing functions, where one function's output is used as input to the other.  Examples:  * `Function.comp List.reverse (List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  * `(List.reverse ∘ List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∘` in identifiers is `comp`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.comp ;)
Function__comp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (W : Set) -> (f : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem W.

(; doc: `f` has the derivative `f'` at the point `x`.  That is, `f x' = f x + (x' - x) • f' + o(x' - x)` where `x'` converges to `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasDerivAt ;)
HasDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (f' : Elem F) -> (x4 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:HasDerivAt.continuousAt ;)
HasDerivAt__continuousAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (f' : Elem F) -> (x3 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (HasDerivAt X F f f' x3)) -> Proof (ContinuousAt X F f x3).

(; //LEAN:HasDerivAt.hasDerivWithinAt ;)
HasDerivAt__hasDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (f' : Elem F) -> (x3 : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (h : Proof (HasDerivAt X F f f' x3)) -> Proof (HasDerivWithinAt X F f f' s x3).

(; doc: **Alias** of the forward direction of `hasDerivAt_iff_hasFDerivAt`.  ---  Expressing `HasDerivAt f f' x` in terms of `HasFDerivAt` ;)
(; //LEAN:HasDerivAt.hasFDerivAt ;)
HasDerivAt__hasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (x4 : Elem X) -> (f' : Elem F) -> (h1 : Proof (HasDerivAt X F f f' x4)) -> Proof (HasFDerivAt X X F f (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X F f') x4).

(; doc: `f` has the derivative `f'` at the point `x` within the subset `s`.  That is, `f x' = f x + (x' - x) • f' + o(x' - x)` where `x'` converges to `x` inside `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasDerivWithinAt ;)
HasDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (f' : Elem F) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x4 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: **Alias** of the forward direction of `hasDerivWithinAt_iff_hasFDerivWithinAt`.  ---  Expressing `HasDerivWithinAt f f' s x` in terms of `HasFDerivWithinAt` ;)
(; //LEAN:HasDerivWithinAt.hasFDerivWithinAt ;)
HasDerivWithinAt__hasFDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (x4 : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (f' : Elem F) -> (h1 : Proof (HasDerivWithinAt X F f f' s x4)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X X F f (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X F f') s x4).

(; //LEAN:HasDerivWithinAt.liminf_right_slope_le ;)
HasDerivWithinAt__liminf_right_slope_le : (f : (Elem Real -> Real)) -> (f' : Elem Real) -> (x : Elem Real) -> (r : Elem Real) -> (hf : Proof (HasDerivWithinAt Real Real f f' (Set__Ici Real x) x)) -> (hr : Proof (Lt f' r)) -> Proof (Filter__Frequently Real (z => Lt (slope Real Real Real f x z) r) (nhdsWithin Real x (Set__Ioi Real x))).

(; doc: If `f` has derivative `f'` within `(x, +∞)` at `x`, then for any `r > ‖f'‖` the ratio `(‖f z‖ - ‖f x‖) / (z - x)` is frequently less than `r` as `z → x+0`. In other words, the limit inferior of this ratio as `z` tends to `x+0` is less than or equal to `‖f'‖`.  See also  * `HasDerivWithinAt.limsup_norm_slope_le` for a stronger version using   limit superior and any set `s`; * `HasDerivWithinAt.liminf_right_norm_slope_le` for a stronger version using   `‖f z - f xp‖` instead of `‖f z‖ - ‖f x‖`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasDerivWithinAt.liminf_right_slope_norm_le ;)
HasDerivWithinAt__liminf_right_slope_norm_le : (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace Real E)) -> (f : (Elem Real -> E)) -> (f' : Elem E) -> (x2 : Elem Real) -> (r : Elem Real) -> (hf : Proof (HasDerivWithinAt Real E f f' (Set__Ici Real x2) x2)) -> (hr : Proof (Lt (Norm__norm E f') r)) -> Proof (Filter__Frequently Real (z => Lt (times (Inv__inv Real (minus z x2)) (minus (Norm__norm E (f z)) (Norm__norm E (f x2)))) r) (nhdsWithin Real x2 (Set__Ioi Real x2))).

(; doc: A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative at `x` if `f x' = f x + f' (x' - x) + o (x' - x)` when `x'` tends to `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt ;)
HasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt.hasFDerivWithinAt ;)
HasFDerivAt__hasFDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7).

(; doc: A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative at `x` within a set `s` if `f x' = f x + f' (x' - x) + o (x' - x)` when `x'` tends to `x` inside `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; doc: `f` has the derivative `f'` at the point `x` in the sense of strict differentiability.  That is, `f y - f z = (y - z) • f' + o(y - z)` as `y, z → x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasStrictDerivAt ;)
HasStrictDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (f' : Elem F) -> (x4 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f` has derivative `f'` at `a` in the sense of *strict differentiability* if `f x - f y - f' (x - y) = o(x - y)` as `x, y → a`. This form of differentiability is required, e.g., by the inverse function theorem. Any `C^1` function on a vector space over `ℝ` is strictly differentiable but this definition works, e.g., for vector spaces over `p`-adic numbers. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasStrictFDerivAt ;)
HasStrictFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; doc: `a ∩ b` is the intersection of `a` and `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∩` in identifiers is `inter`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inter.inter ;)
Inter__inter : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `a⁻¹` computes the inverse of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⁻¹` in identifiers is `inv`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inv.inv ;)
Inv__inv : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A function between topological spaces is locally constant if the preimage of any set is open. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsLocallyConstant ;)
IsLocallyConstant : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `IsOpen s` means that `s` is open in the ambient topological space on `X` ;)
(; //LEAN:IsOpen ;)
IsOpen : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A preconnected set is one where there is no non-trivial open partition. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsPreconnected ;)
IsPreconnected : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: **Alias** of `lt_of_le_of_lt`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LE.le.trans_lt ;)
LE__le__trans_lt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (hab : Proof (Leq a b)) -> (hbc : Proof (Lt b c)) -> Proof (Lt a c).

(; doc: A function `f` is **Lipschitz continuous** with constant `K ≥ 0` **on `s`** if for all `x, y` in `s` we have `dist (f x) (f y) ≤ K * dist x y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LipschitzOnWith ;)
LipschitzOnWith : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (K : Elem NNReal) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f` is **Lipschitz continuous** with constant `K ≥ 0` if for all `x, y` we have `dist (f x) (f y) ≤ K * dist x y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LipschitzWith ;)
LipschitzWith : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (K : Elem NNReal) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The membership relation `a ∈ s : Prop` where `a : α`, `s : γ`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∈` in identifiers is `mem`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Membership.mem ;)
Membership__mem : (X : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem Z) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: the `ℝ≥0`-valued norm function. ;)
(; //LEAN:NNNorm.nnnorm ;)
NNNorm__nnnorm : (E : Set) -> (x : Elem E) -> Elem NNReal.

(; doc: Nonnegative real numbers, denoted as `ℝ≥0` within the NNReal namespace ;)
(; //LEAN:NNReal ;)
NNReal : Set.

(; doc: For each term `a`, there is some strictly greater `b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:NoMaxOrder.exists_gt ;)
NoMaxOrder__exists_gt : (X : Set) -> (x : Proof (LT X)) -> (_ : Proof (NoMaxOrder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (exists X (b => Lt a b)).

(; doc: A nontrivially normed field is a normed field in which there is an element of norm different from `0` and `1`. This makes it possible to bring any element arbitrarily close to `0` by multiplication by the powers of any element, and thus to relate algebra and topology. ;)
(; //LEAN:NontriviallyNormedField ;)
NontriviallyNormedField : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: the `ℝ`-valued norm function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Norm.norm ;)
Norm__norm : (E : Set) -> (x : Elem E) -> Elem Real.

(; doc: A normed group is an additive group endowed with a norm for which `dist x y = ‖-x + y‖` defines a metric space structure. ;)
(; //LEAN:NormedAddCommGroup ;)
NormedAddCommGroup : (E : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A normed space over a normed field is a vector space endowed with a norm which satisfies the equality `‖c • x‖ = ‖c‖ ‖x‖`. We require only `‖c • x‖ ≤ ‖c‖ ‖x‖` in the definition, then prove `‖c • x‖ = ‖c‖ ‖x‖` in `norm_smul`.  Note that since this requires `SeminormedAddCommGroup` and not `NormedAddCommGroup`, this typeclass can be used for "seminormed spaces" too, just as `Module` can be used for "semimodules". ;)
(; //LEAN:NormedSpace ;)
NormedSpace : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Preorder.toLT ;)
Preorder__toLT : (X : Set) -> Proof (LT X).

(; doc: This typeclass captures properties shared by ℝ and ℂ, with an API that closely matches that of ℂ. ;)
(; //LEAN:RCLike ;)
RCLike : (K : Elem Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The identity ring homomorphism from a semiring to itself. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHom.id ;)
RingHom__id : (X : Set) -> Elem (RingHom X X).

(; doc: Two functions `f₁ f₂ : α → β` are equal on `s` if `f₁ x = f₂ x` for all `x ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.EqOn ;)
Set__EqOn : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x1 : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Icc a b` is the left-closed right-closed interval $[a, b]$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Icc ;)
Set__Icc : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `Ici a` is the left-closed right-infinite interval $[a, ∞)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Ici ;)
Set__Ici : (X : Set) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `Ico a b` is the left-closed right-open interval $[a, b)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Ico ;)
Set__Ico : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `Ioi a` is the left-open right-infinite interval $(a, ∞)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Ioi ;)
Set__Ioi : (X : Set) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: The preimage of `s : Set β` by `f : α → β`, written `f ⁻¹' s`, is the set of `x : α` such that `f x ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.preimage ;)
Set__preimage : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset Y)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: The universal set on a type `α` is the set containing all elements of `α`.  This is conceptually the "same as" `α` (in set theory, it is actually the same), but type theory makes the distinction that `α` is a type while `Set.univ` is a term of type `Set α`. `Set.univ` can itself be coerced to a type `↥Set.univ` which is in bijection with (but distinct from) `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.univ ;)
Set__univ : (X : Set) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; //LEAN:Std.le_refl._simp_1 ;)
Std__le_refl___simp_1 : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (LE X)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Refl X (x1 => x2 => Leq x1 x2))) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Leq a a) True).

(; doc: The *strong dual* of a topological vector space `M` over a ring `R`. This is the space of continuous linear functionals and is equipped with the topology of uniform convergence on bounded subsets. `StrongDual R M` is an abbreviation for `M →L[R] R`. ;)
(; //LEAN:StrongDual ;)
StrongDual : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; doc: A property ensuring that the tangent cone to `s` at any of its points spans a dense subset of the whole space. The main role of this property is to ensure that the differential along `s` is unique, hence this name. The uniqueness it asserts is proved in `UniqueDiffOn.eq` in `Mathlib/Analysis/Calculus/FDeriv/Basic.lean`. ;)
(; //LEAN:UniqueDiffOn ;)
UniqueDiffOn : (R : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A property ensuring that the tangent cone to `s` at `x` spans a dense subset of the whole space. The main role of this property is to ensure that the differential within `s` at `x` is unique, hence this name. The uniqueness it asserts is proved in `UniqueDiffWithinAt.eq` in `Mathlib/Analysis/Calculus/FDeriv/Basic.lean`. To avoid pathologies in dimension 0, we also require that `x` belongs to the closure of `s` (which is automatic when `E` is not `0`-dimensional). ;)
(; //LEAN:UniqueDiffWithinAt ;)
UniqueDiffWithinAt : (R : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x4 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: Similar to `congrFun` but `β` does not depend on `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congrFun' ;)
congrFun' : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) f g)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f a) (g a)).

(; //LEAN:continuous_norm ;)
continuous_norm : (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddGroup E)) -> Proof (Continuous E Real (a => Norm__norm E a)).

(; //LEAN:convex_univ ;)
convex_univ : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> (_ : Proof (PartialOrder X)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid E)) -> Proof (Convex X E (Set__univ E)).

(; doc: Derivative of `f` at the point `x`, if it exists.  Zero otherwise.  If the derivative exists (i.e., `∃ f', HasDerivAt f f' x`), then `f x' = f x + (x' - x) • deriv f x + o(x' - x)` where `x'` converges to `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:deriv ;)
deriv : (X : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (x4 : Elem X) -> Elem F.

(; doc: Derivative of `f` at the point `x` within the set `s`, if it exists.  Zero otherwise.  If the derivative exists (i.e., `∃ f', HasDerivWithinAt f f' s x`), then `f x' = f x + (x' - x) • derivWithin f s x + o(x' - x)` where `x'` converges to `x` inside `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:derivWithin ;)
derivWithin : (X : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x4 : Elem X) -> Elem F.

(; //LEAN:eq_comm ;)
eq_comm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Eqt X a b) (Eqt X b a)).

(; doc: If `f` has a derivative at `x`, then `fderiv 𝕜 f x` is such a derivative. Otherwise, it is set to `0`. ;)
(; //LEAN:fderiv ;)
fderiv : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F).

(; doc: If `f` has a derivative at `x` within `s`, then `fderivWithin 𝕜 f s x` is such a derivative. Otherwise, it is set to `0`. We also set it to be zero, if zero is one of possible derivatives. ;)
(; //LEAN:fderivWithin ;)
fderivWithin : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F).

(; //LEAN:fderivWithin_sub ;)
fderivWithin_sub : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (g : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (hxs : Proof (UniqueDiffWithinAt X E s x5)) -> (hf : Proof (DifferentiableWithinAt X E F f s x5)) -> (hg : Proof (DifferentiableWithinAt X E F g s x5)) -> Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F) (fderivWithin X E F (minus f g) s x5) (minus (fderivWithin X E F f s x5) (fderivWithin X E F g s x5))).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: The relation `≤` on a preorder is transitive. ;)
(; //LEAN:le_trans ;)
le_trans : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Leq a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Leq b c)) -> Proof (Leq a c).

(; doc: `f` is Lipschitz iff it is Lipschitz on the entire space. ;)
(; //LEAN:lipschitzOnWith_univ ;)
lipschitzOnWith_univ : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (PseudoEMetricSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (PseudoEMetricSpace Y)) -> (K : Elem NNReal) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Proof (iff (LipschitzOnWith X Y K f (Set__univ X)) (LipschitzWith X Y K f)).

(; //LEAN:lt_of_le_of_lt ;)
lt_of_le_of_lt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (hab : Proof (Leq a b)) -> (hbc : Proof (Lt b c)) -> Proof (Lt a c).

(; doc: A set is called a neighborhood of `x` if it contains an open set around `x`. The set of all neighborhoods of `x` forms a filter, the neighborhood filter at `x`, is here defined as the infimum over the principal filters of all open sets containing `x`.  [Wikidata Q3275652](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3275652) ;)
(; //LEAN:nhds ;)
nhds : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem (Filter X).

(; doc: The "neighborhood within" filter. Elements of `𝓝[s] x` are sets containing the intersection of `s` and a neighborhood of `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:nhdsWithin ;)
nhdsWithin : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (Filter X).

(; //LEAN:of_eq_true ;)
of_eq_true : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Prop p True)) -> Proof p.

(; doc: The [axiom](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=axioms) of **propositional extensionality**. It asserts that if propositions `a` and `b` are logically equivalent (that is, if `a` can be proved from `b` and vice versa), then `a` and `b` are *equal*, meaning `a` can be replaced with `b` in all contexts.  The standard logical connectives provably respect propositional extensionality. However, an axiom is needed for higher order expressions like `P a` where `P : Prop → Prop` is unknown, as well as for equality. Propositional extensionality is intuitionistically valid. ;)
(; //LEAN:propext ;)
propext : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop a b).

(; doc: `slope f a b = (b - a)⁻¹ • (f b -ᵥ f a)` is the slope of a function `f` on the interval `[a, b]`. Note that `slope f a a = 0`, not the derivative of `f` at `a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:slope ;)
slope : (k : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (PE : Set) -> (f : (Elem k -> PE)) -> (a : Elem k) -> (b : Elem k) -> Elem E.

(; //LEAN:sub_eq_zero ;)
sub_eq_zero : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (iff (Eqt G (minus a b) 0) (Eqt G a b)).

(; //LEAN:sub_self ;)
sub_self : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (minus a a) 0).

